WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.267
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level four.

2
00:00:06.567 --> 00:00:10.244
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.544 --> 00:00:16.192
Unit eighteen: Global Public Health. Today you will describe data and trends precisely.

4
00:00:16.492 --> 00:00:19.051
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.198
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.550
Disease eradication.

7
00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:30.396
Disease eradication means the complete removal of a disease from the world.

8
00:00:30.983 --> 00:00:33.679
Disease eradication ended smallpox.

9
00:00:34.283 --> 00:00:37.574
Your turn. Say: Disease eradication.

10
00:00:40.433 --> 00:00:41.945
Disease eradication.

11
00:00:42.683 --> 00:00:44.381
Vaccination campaign.

12
00:00:45.383 --> 00:00:50.599
Vaccination campaign means an organised effort to vaccinate large numbers of people.

13
00:00:51.183 --> 00:00:54.205
A vaccination campaign reached remote villages.

14
00:00:54.817 --> 00:00:58.282
Your turn. Say: Vaccination campaign.

15
00:01:01.133 --> 00:01:02.843
Vaccination campaign.

16
00:01:03.583 --> 00:01:05.351
Healthcare infrastructure.

17
00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:12.169
Healthcare infrastructure means the clinics, hospitals, roads and equipment that support health services.

18
00:01:12.783 --> 00:01:15.990
Healthcare infrastructure improved in rural areas.

19
00:01:16.583 --> 00:01:19.921
Your turn. Say: Healthcare infrastructure.

20
00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:24.650
Healthcare infrastructure.

21
00:01:25.417 --> 00:01:26.778
Epidemiology.

22
00:01:27.783 --> 00:01:31.838
Epidemiology means the study of how diseases spread in populations.

23
00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.158
Epidemiology showed where the outbreak began.

24
00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:38.414
Your turn. Say: Epidemiology.

25
00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:42.651
Epidemiology.

26
00:01:43.417 --> 00:01:44.871
Global health fund.

27
00:01:45.883 --> 00:01:50.228
Global health fund means an international pool of money used to fight disease.

28
00:01:50.817 --> 00:01:53.350
A global health fund paid for the medicines.

29
00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:57.171
Your turn. Say: Global health fund.

30
00:02:00.033 --> 00:02:01.882
Global health fund.

31
00:02:02.617 --> 00:02:04.140
Health workforce.

32
00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:10.017
Health workforce means all the doctors, nurses and community workers in a health system.

33
00:02:10.617 --> 00:02:12.802
The health workforce needs more training.

34
00:02:13.417 --> 00:02:16.708
Your turn. Say: Health workforce.

35
00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:21.090
Health workforce.

36
00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:23.327
Preventable disease.

37
00:02:24.317 --> 00:02:29.091
Preventable disease means an illness that can be stopped by vaccines or basic care.

38
00:02:29.683 --> 00:02:31.718
Measles is a preventable disease.

39
00:02:32.317 --> 00:02:35.201
Your turn. Say: Preventable disease.

40
00:02:38.067 --> 00:02:39.451
Preventable disease.

41
00:02:40.217 --> 00:02:41.961
Health system strengthening.

42
00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:47.179
Health system strengthening means improving the structures that deliver health care.

43
00:02:47.783 --> 00:02:50.932
Health system strengthening saves lives for decades.

44
00:02:51.517 --> 00:02:54.831
Your turn. Say: Health system strengthening.

45
00:02:57.667 --> 00:02:59.794
Health system strengthening.

46
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.030
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:04.883 --> 00:03:12.946
Visal, a report says 'Child deaths fell by two thirds, accounting for millions of saved lives.' How do I describe statistics like this?

48
00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:25.526
Use precise verbs and fractions: fell by two thirds, doubled, rose sharply, accounted for 40 percent. A threefold increase means three times as much.

49
00:03:26.617 --> 00:03:28.779
What is the difference between by and to?

50
00:03:29.883 --> 00:03:42.224
'Cases fell by half' shows the size of the change. 'Cases fell to 5,000' shows the new level. 'From 10,000 to 5,000' shows both. The meanings are not the same.

51
00:03:43.317 --> 00:03:45.049
Any tips for comparison?

52
00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:57.510
Use 'twice as many', 'three times higher than', 'a fraction of' and 'the vast majority of'. Add a source, such as 'according to the data', to sound reliable.

53
00:03:58.667 --> 00:04:00.167
Part three: grammar.

54
00:04:01.017 --> 00:04:06.453
Grammar focus: Describing data: fell by and rose to and accounted for.

55
00:04:06.753 --> 00:04:08.044
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:11.597
Child deaths fell by two thirds in thirty years.

57
00:04:12.483 --> 00:04:15.748
Vaccination coverage rose to ninety percent of children.

58
00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:19.961
Preventable disease accounted for nearly half of all deaths.

59
00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:29.984
By shows the size of a change (fell by half); to shows the new level (fell to 5,000); accounted for shows a share. Add a source to sound credible.

60
00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:35.097
Be careful! Don't say: Deaths accounted by half of cases.

61
00:04:37.033 --> 00:04:39.973
Say: Deaths accounted for half of cases.

62
00:04:40.273 --> 00:04:42.366
Use 'accounted for' to show a share.

63
00:04:43.167 --> 00:04:45.923
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:46.733 --> 00:04:48.060
Reading the Numbers.

65
00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:52.844
Epidemiology uses data to show how disease spreads and how it can be stopped.

66
00:04:53.833 --> 00:05:01.882
A vaccination campaign can raise coverage from half of children to nearly all, and cases of a preventable disease may fall by ninety percent.

67
00:05:02.867 --> 00:05:07.792
Disease eradication is rare, but smallpox was removed after cases fell to zero.

68
00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:16.442
A global health fund, together with a strong health workforce and better healthcare infrastructure, accounts for much of this progress.

69
00:05:17.433 --> 00:05:23.009
Health system strengthening matters because gains can disappear if funding falls by even a small amount.

70
00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:27.974
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:28.817 --> 00:05:32.198
Cases fell blank half, from 800 to 400.

72
00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:35.397
A, by. B, to. C, at.

73
00:05:38.933 --> 00:05:40.515
The answer is A: by.

74
00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:44.660
Cases fell blank 400, from 800.

75
00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:47.893
A, by. B, to. C, for.

76
00:05:51.433 --> 00:05:53.143
The answer is B: to.

77
00:05:54.283 --> 00:05:56.898
A threefold increase means the number became blank.

78
00:05:57.198 --> 00:06:01.421
A, three times as large. B, one third. C, three more.

79
00:06:04.967 --> 00:06:08.232
The answer is A: three times as large.

80
00:06:09.383 --> 00:06:12.637
The study of how diseases spread in populations is blank.

81
00:06:12.937 --> 00:06:17.786
A, epidemiology. B, diplomacy. C, irrigation.

82
00:06:21.333 --> 00:06:23.577
The answer is A: epidemiology.

83
00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:28.760
According to the reading, what made smallpox eradication possible?

84
00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:34.222
A, cases fell to zero. B, tariffs rose. C, a new oil treaty.

85
00:06:37.767 --> 00:06:40.765
The answer is A: cases fell to zero.

86
00:06:41.967 --> 00:06:44.967
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:45.817 --> 00:06:50.103
What has been one of the greatest successes in the history of global public health?

88
00:06:58.917 --> 00:07:00.324
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:00.624 --> 00:07:28.473
One of the greatest successes is the eradication of smallpox, which was declared over in 1980. A worldwide vaccination campaign, careful disease surveillance and cooperation between countries cut the cases to zero. It killed millions of people in earlier centuries, so its end shows what a global health fund, trained health workers and shared data can achieve.

90
00:07:28.773 --> 00:07:30.540
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:38.468
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and describing data: fell by and rose to and accounted for.

92
00:07:38.768 --> 00:07:43.626
Today's value is evidence-based judgment. Let good evidence guide what you decide.

93
00:07:43.926 --> 00:07:46.660
See you in Unit nineteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.542
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

